WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.000
This is Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedanta. In October 2014, Psychology Professor Nancy Siegel heard a story. It was about her specialty, the study of twins. Nancy had made a career of studying unusual cases of twins, but she had never heard a story like this one. It's December 1988. A woman from a remote farming village in Colombia makes a day-long trek to a hospital. She gives birth there to a pair of identical twin boys.
这里是《隐藏的大脑》，我是尚卡尔·维丹塔姆。2014年10月，心理学教授南希·西格尔听到了一个故事。故事关于她的专业领域——双胞胎研究。南希职业生涯中研究过许多罕见的双胞胎案例，但她从未听过这样的故事。时间回到1988年12月。哥伦比亚一个偏远农村的一名妇女，跋涉了一整天来到医院。她在那里生下了一对同卵双胞胎男孩。

2
00:00:37.000 --> 00:01:08.000
One of the twins needs more advanced medical care that isn't available. One of them was extremely sick. Since the mom is still recuperating, a relative takes him to another hospital, six hours away in Bogota, the capital. At the Bogota Hospital, another set of identical twin boys has just been born. Their mother is a seamstress who lives in the city. The scene was quite chaotic. The hospital staff is tired and overworked.
其中一个双胞胎需要更先进的医疗护理，但当地没有。其中一个病得非常重。由于母亲还在恢复期，一位亲戚带着他去了六小时路程外的首都波哥大的另一家医院。在波哥大的那家医院，刚好也出生了一对同卵双胞胎男孩。他们的母亲是一位住在城里的裁缝。当时现场非常混乱。医院工作人员既疲惫又超负荷工作。

3
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:48.000
It was approaching Christmas time. People were thinking about the holidays. Now, nobody knows exactly what happens next, but something goes wrong. The baby from the countryside is switched with one of the babies born to the mother from the city. By the time all the babies are sent home, one boy from each pair of identical twins is in the wrong family. Each family thinks their twins are fraternal. The homes the boys grow up in are in totally different places, 150 miles away from each
当时临近圣诞节。人们都在想着过节的事。现在，没人确切知道接下来发生了什么，但出了差错。来自农村的婴儿与来自城里的母亲所生的婴儿之一被调换了。当所有婴儿被送回家时，每对同卵双胞胎中都有一个男孩被送到了错误的家庭。每个家庭都以为他们的双胞胎是异卵的。男孩们长大的地方完全不同，相距150英里，

4
00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:27.000
other, and a world apart. One the lively capital city of Bogota, and the other in a very remote farming village, and they'd had no modern amenities, no schooling, things of that sort. Researchers who studied twins knew of many examples of identical twins raised apart. But two sets of identical twins, where one from each pair was switched into the other home, it had never been documented before. It brought up fascinating questions. 25 years later, when the truth was discovered, Nancy says it was also deeply painful.
彼此之间，简直是天壤之别。一个在繁华的首都波哥大，另一个在非常偏远的农村，那里没有现代设施，没有学校，诸如此类。研究双胞胎的学者知道许多同卵双胞胎分开抚养的例子。但两对同卵双胞胎，每对中各有一个被换到了另一个家庭，这在以前从未被记录过。这引出了引人入胜的问题。25年后，当真相被发现时，南希说这也令人深感痛苦。

5
00:02:27.000 --> 00:03:10.000
It was also a tragedy in the sense that two of the twins grew up in a home where they were not biologically connected, and they discovered in a matter of minutes that their identities were totally shattered. They weren't to their thought they were, they belonged in other families, their parents were not their parents. In short, everything about their life was basically a line. This week we'll delve into the story of the Bogota twins, a tragedy, a soap opera, and a science experiment all rolled into one. And we'll also find out why twin studies can act as a natural experiment to test some of the biggest questions about human nature.
从某种意义上说，这也是一场悲剧，因为其中两个双胞胎在没有血缘关系的家庭中长大，他们在几分钟内发现自己的身份完全破碎了。他们不是他们以为的那样，他们属于其他家庭，他们的父母不是他们的父母。简而言之，他们生活的一切基本上都是谎言。本周，我们将深入探讨波哥大双胞胎的故事，这是一个集悲剧、肥皂剧和科学实验于一体的故事。我们还将了解为什么双胞胎研究可以作为一种自然实验，来测试关于人性的一些重大问题。

6
00:03:10.000 --> 00:04:05.000
How much of our lives is shaped by upbringing? How much by genes? Are we the masters of our own fate? Or are we all destined to become our mothers? Insights and provocations from two in-studies this week on Hidden Brain. Nancy Siegel is a professor of psychology at California State University, Fulitan. She is the director of that twin study center. She wrote about the Bogota twins in her book, Accidental Brothers, The Story of Twins Exchanged at Birth, and the power of nature and nurture.
我们的生活有多少是由教养塑造的？有多少是由基因决定的？我们是自己命运的主宰吗？还是我们注定要成为我们的母亲？本周在《隐藏的大脑》中，我们将从双胞胎研究中获得见解和启发。南希·西格尔是加州州立大学富勒顿分校的心理学教授。她是该双胞胎研究中心的主任。她在她的书《意外的兄弟：出生时被交换的双胞胎的故事，以及先天与后天的力量》中写到了波哥大双胞胎。

7
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:38.000
In both the remote farming village and the city, each set of mismatched brothers had very different outlooks and personalities. In each family, one brother seemed outgoing, the other reserved. And in fact, people would kid the twins and the parents and say, are you sure your twins? And how could you have two boys look so different? But people again are sensitive to fraternal twin differences. People know that ordinary siblings and a family who have the same genetic relatedness as fraternal twins often look and act very differently.
在偏远的农村和城市里，每对错位的兄弟都有着非常不同的性格和人生观。在每个家庭中，一个兄弟似乎外向，另一个内向。事实上，人们会开玩笑地问双胞胎和父母，你们确定是双胞胎吗？你们怎么会有两个长相如此不同的男孩？但人们对异卵双胞胎的差异很敏感。人们知道，家庭中拥有相同遗传相关性的普通兄弟姐妹，看起来和表现往往也非常不同。

